📝 Main Topics
1. AWS Pipeline Flow and Repository Separation
Background:
The deployment flow uses multiple connected processes. The MDTC shared library pipeline is a Jenkins pipeline that publishes an artifact, runs Bornify and Black Duck, and documents the artifact contents. MDTCV2 handles CFTs, while AWS CodePipeline retrieves shared-library artifacts and related Docker, Python, and NPM dependencies from Artifactory, starts CodeBuild, and executes make setup.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
The current AWS flow is understood as follows: DPCD2 triggers AWS CodePipeline; CodePipeline starts CodeBuild; CodeBuild retrieves required resources, including items from Secrets Manager and NBM9 from Artifactory; the final script invokes make setup. The team confirmed that make runs in AWS rather than on an individual’s local machine.
The team also agreed that pipeline implementation and execution materials should be maintained separately from product code where possible, allowing independent changes, change tracking, semantic versioning, and planning while reducing merge conflicts.
Responsibility and Actions/Follow-up Actions:
Rob is working on the pipeline, while Topaz is working on the template.
The team will compare the current copied repo with the original AWS source repo before finalizing repository boundaries.
The pipeline structure will be reviewed to confirm which components belong to CodePipeline, the shared library, and the product repo.
Unresolved Matters:
The team has not confirmed whether all pipeline-related components have already been added to the current repo or whether the current repository represents the original AWS source accurately.
2. Makefile Availability and Deployment Responsibility
Background:
The team expected the downloaded shared-library artifact to contain a root-level Makefile and dependent sub-Makefiles, but the extracted ZIP appeared not to contain a Makefile. The repository itself is believed to contain the expected Makefile.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
The Makefile is embedded in and invoked by the CodePipeline process. However, the Makefile was observed performing stack operations, while the stated expectation was that CodePipeline should perform stack deployments. This responsibility boundary requires investigation rather than assuming that the current arrangement is final.
Responsibility and Actions/Follow-up Actions:
The Makefile execution and packaging path will be inspected.
The original AWS source repo will be compared with the current repo to determine whether the stack-performing Makefile or related files were added, omitted, or modified.
The team will determine whether the Makefile should be delivered through the shared link, published through Artifactory or S3, or supplied through another pipeline mechanism.
Unresolved Matters:
The source of the missing Makefile in the downloaded artifact and the final ownership of stack operations remain unresolved.
